/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  dryer03_solid_temperature  --  dryer01_sucrose_tray with the SOLID'S TEMPERATURE
  integrated (C512).  Everything below about the air, the wet bulb, the break and the
  moisture is dryer01's, unchanged; what is new is `solidTemperature integrated;` and
  its KPIs (T_solid_final, solidEnergy_*), the trajectory column dryer.T_solid_K.

  WHAT THE SOLID DOES (C512; Theory Guide sec:batch-dryer-solid-T):
    * X > X_c: T_s IS the wet bulb, 300.52 K (dryer01's), held (hypothesis 2).
      h = k_Y c_H = 0.05 * 1060.2 = 53.01 W/(m2 K) (Lewis = 1: the relation the
      wet-bulb equation is written in), so at the wet bulb h A (T_air - T_wb) =
      R_c A lambda(T_wb) = 0.8648 kW: the heat in equals the latent load, and the
      temperature leaves the wet bulb continuously at X_c.
    * X <= X_c: C(X) dT_s/dt = h A (T_air - T_s) - R A lambda(T_s), C = m_s (cp_s +
      X cp_w), cp_s = 1243 J/(kg K) (the record's 425.5 J/(mol K)), cp_w = 4191.
      The time scale of the warm-up is C/(h A) = 100-130 s, short against the
      falling-rate time tau = 595 s, so T_s follows the air with a lag and
      T_air - T_s decays with the drying curve (T_air - T_s ~ 1.4 K at 3000 s).
    * LIMIT, by hand: X -> X_eq makes R -> 0 and T_s -> T_air (333.15 K); at 3000 s
      X = 0.01963 against X_eq = 0.01586, 3.6 % of the free moisture left, and
      T_s = 331.78 K, 1.37 K below the air.
    * INDEPENDENT CHECK: the same two equations integrated outside the engine
      (a ten-line RK4 with the printed constants and the Watson latent heat) end
      at T_s = 331.7796 K against the engine's 331.7787 K, X = 0.019628 against
      0.019631.
    * THE ENERGY LEDGER closes: E_conv = 1470.48 kJ in from the air, E_out =
      1387.63 kJ out with the vapour (latent + its sensible), and the solid
      stores (m_s cp_s + m_w cp_w)(T_s - T_wb) = 2650.7 J/K * 31.26 K = 82.85
      kJ: 1470.48 - 1387.63 - 82.85 = 0 to 1.1e-9 of E_conv (KPI
      solidEnergy_closure_rel).  The campaign's own energy balance stays
      unavailable (the air is outside it), as in dryer01.
    * Announced assumptions: R is the characteristic drying curve and does not
      read T_s; one temperature for the lumped solid (small Biot); cp at the
      mean of T_wb and T_air; the charge starts at T_wb.

  A tray holding 2.0 kg of dry sucrose wet to X_0 = 0.30 kg water / kg dry
  solid, exposed over 0.5 m2 to air of DECLARED, CONSTANT condition:
  60 C (333.15 K) and Y = 0.010 kg water / kg dry N2.  Nothing about that
  air is integrated -- it is an ENVIRONMENT, the FixedBedAdsorber's
  constant-carrier posture, and the water that leaves the solid leaves the
  campaign across that boundary (reported as the unit's DECLARED material
  residual, never as a silent leak).

  THE HAND ESTIMATE, computed from this case's own curated records
  (water Antoine + Watson Hvap + the two ideal-gas Cp polynomials, the
  case-local GAB isotherm on sucrose) -- the run must reproduce it:

    air water activity      p_v = P Y/(Y + Mv/Mc) = 1551 Pa
                            a_w = p_v / Psat(333.15 K) = 0.0774
    GAB equilibrium         K a_w = 0.0743
                            X_eq  = Xm C Ka /((1-Ka)(1-Ka+C Ka)) = 0.01580 kg/kg
    wet bulb (Lewis = 1)    T_wb  = 300.53 K   (27.4 C, a 32.6 K depression)
    driving force           Y_sat(T_wb) - Y    = 0.014011 kg/kg
    constant-rate flux      R_c = k_Y dY = 0.05 * 0.014011
                                = 7.0057e-4 kg/(m2 s)
                            R_c A          = 3.5028e-4 kg/s
    latent load             R_c A lambda(T_wb) = 0.8645 kW  (the AIR's, not
                                the campaign's -- KPI only, never ledgered)
    break time              t_c = m_s (X_0 - X_c)/(R_c A) = 1027.7 s
    falling-rate constant   tau = m_s (X_c - X_eq)/(R_c A) = 595.0 s
    end of run              X(3000 s) = X_eq + (X_c - X_eq) exp(-(3000-t_c)/tau)
                                      = 0.019584 kg/kg

  What to look at in the output:
    * trajectory.csv columns dryer.X_kg_kg and dryer.R_kg_m2_s: the drying
      curve and the rate curve, engine columns, not a post-processing.
      R is FLAT at 7.0057e-4 until X falls through X_c = 0.12, then decays
      linearly in (X - X_eq) -- the textbook break, visible as a corner.
      The falling rate is DECLARED as the characteristic drying curve
      f = Phi^n with n = 1, which IS the linear law; the drying EduTool
      varies n (n < 1 reaches X_eq in finite time, n > 1 an algebraic tail
      slower than the exponential), and dryer02_characteristic_curves runs
      four shapes side by side.
    * the KPI t_critical is OBSERVED (the interpolated crossing between two
      accepted states), not the analytic 1027.7 s above; they agree because
      dX/dt is exactly constant on that leg.
    * THE CAMPAIGN MASS BALANCE CLOSES BECAUSE THE DRIED WATER IS AN OUTLET.
      The solid is charged with 2.600000 kg and ends with 2.039168 kg; the
      0.560832 kg of difference is the DRIED WATER.  It left the solid and
      went into the air, and the campaign books it as an external OUTLET
      STREAM of the dryer (the ledger record `dryer -> (external boundary)`,
      "evaporated water leaving the solid into the drying air"): initial =
      final + external out, mass and element closure at round-off.  The
      air stays a DECLARED ENVIRONMENT (a temperature and a humidity, not a
      vessel this campaign owns), but matter crossing the campaign boundary
      is a stream whatever receives it.  Until 2026-11-09 this unit left the
      water off the books and the balance read a 21.6 % shortfall that only
      the log explained; a violated balance on screen is a defect, so it is
      a stream now (DEV.md 4c C258).

    * the campaign energy balance is UNAVAILABLE, and says why in one
      sentence: the drying heat comes from air this campaign never owned.
      The number is not hidden -- it is the latentDuty_kW KPI.

  Method hypotheses, each announced at run time: Lewis = 1 in the
  adiabatic-saturation equation; the humid heat evaluated at the air's own
  temperature; the surface held at T_wb while X > X_c; the LINEAR
  falling-rate curve (n = 1; a modelling CHOICE, not physics, and its
  declared source says so); the solid's own
  temperature NOT integrated.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// the vessel's initial holdup lives in 0/internalStates/dryer (the single source
// of truth); flowsheetDict carries the topology and the operation only

units
(
    {
        name        dryer;
        type        batchDryer;

        operation
        {
            area              0.5 m2;       // exposed tray surface
            k_Y               [1 -2 -1 0 0]  0.05;
                                            // gas-film coefficient,
                                            // kg/(m2 s) per unit humidity
                                            // ratio -- SAMPLE/EQUIPMENT data
                                            // (air velocity, tray geometry)
            criticalMoisture  0.12;         // kg/kg dry solid -- MEASURED

            solidTemperature  integrated;   // OPT-IN: the lumped solid's energy balance in the
                                            // falling-rate period (absent: held at the wet bulb)

            dryingCurve                     // f = R/R_c against Phi =
            {                               // (X - X_eq)/(X_c - X_eq)
                shape     power;
                exponent  1.0;              // n = 1: the LINEAR curve
                source    "the linear curve, written as f = Phi^n with n = 1 so the drying EduTool can vary n -- a hypothetical teaching curve, not a measured material";
            }

            air                             // a DECLARED environment,
            {                               // not a ledgered stream
                T        333.15 K;
                Y        0.010;             // kg water / kg dry carrier
                carrier  N2;
            }
        }
    }
);
